## Source editions
- [ ] For every text, record the exact edition it was transcribed from (not just the original work) in the collection's Chicago reference
- [ ] Novum Organum (n1fx2): confirm which English translation was used. Spedding/Ellis/Heath (1858) is fine; Urbach & Gibson (1994) or Jardine & Silverthorne (2000) would be in copyright
- [ ] Hume, Enquiry (cn2ry) and Dialogues (y5qfr): confirm the copy-text is a pre-1950s printing, not a modern critical edition
- [ ] Alexis, "Don Alexis unto the Reader" (ph63y): the spelling is modernised. Note who modernised it (me, or a named modern edition) and switch to an old printing if needed
- [ ] Nova Kepleriana 4 (713cz): add the public-domain rationale (von Dyck d. 1934, so free in the EU since 2005; Kepler's letters long free)

## Public-domain rationale
- [ ] Add a fixed "why this is public domain" field to every collection (author's death date, or anonymous publication more than 70 years ago)
- [ ] Rewrite the About page answer to "Is this copyright infringement?": every text is in the public domain, and each collection states its source edition and why

## Scans
- [ ] Audit every hosted scan and note where it came from
- [ ] Remove any images downloaded from the British Newspaper Archive, Gale or the Spectator Archive (their terms forbid redistribution); link to their page instead
- [ ] Prefer scans from sources that allow reuse: Delpher, Gallica, Internet Archive, Google Books, Wikimedia Commons, or my own photos of physical copies
- [ ] Store each scan's source and licence in data.json

